<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scene-Level Policy Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer (b), we assess our self-improving fine-tuning strategy using quantitative metrics (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Comparative Study ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Comparative Study ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and qualitative analysis (Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
In quantitative metrics, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> on both CLIP scores and physical plausibility.
The x-axis in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> represents ”rounds of action” within a generation sequence. While GPT-4o (baseline) shows some early refinement, our fine-tuned model demonstrates significantly stronger iterative improvement. Qualitative examples highlight <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span>’s <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scene-Level Policy</span>’s self-correction. In a human study (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Comparative Study ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), five graduate students evaluated 100 room generations (two views each) on prompt alignment, layout coherence, and preference. With five graduate students conducting the evaluation, we find that 3D-Generalist is preferred over Holodeck in approximately 4 out of 5 generations across all three metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We explore how self-improvement fine-tuning impacts VLM performance in general domains. Since our approach trains the VLM to generate better action code from image observations, we hypothesize it enhances visually grounded understanding, transferable to general-domain imagery. To verify this, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span>’s <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scene-Level Policy</span> on two commonly used visual hallucination benchmarks (i.e., <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object HalBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMBER</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>) that directly assess the accuracy of the VLM’s visual grounding capabilities (details in supplementary).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Results in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Asset-Level Policy Evaluation ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show that the base VLM GPT-4o benefits significantly from our self-improvement fine-tuning, with the fine-tuned <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> exhibiting lower hallucination rates across both benchmarks and multiple metric levels, except for a slight decrease in object coverage (COVER) on AMBER. These results demonstrate that, in addition to generating 3D scenes more aligned with the prompt, our self-improvement fine-tuning also benefits VLMs’ general-domain visual understanding, effectively reducing their visual hallucinations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Asset-Level Policy Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, GPT-4o is responsible for determining whether a 3D asset qualifies as a receptacle object,
while another VLM, Molmo-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, provides the visual cues for pixel-level placement.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Self-Improvement Fine-tuning ‣ 3.2 Scene-Level Policy ‣ 3 3D-Generalist ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> 5’s qualitative examples, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asset-Level Policy</span> can place books on shelves, stack plates on tables, or fill open shelves with suitable items.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> quantitatively, we ran the experiment on 15 different “receptacle assets” split equally into three categories: small tables (nightstand, student desk, bar, side table, foyer table), large tables (coffee table, glass coffee table, kitchen countertop, metal table, office desk), and multi-level assets (bookshelf - open back, bookshelf - closed back, pantry shelf, work shelf, warehouse shelf). After finishing placement, we render from four equally spaced camera positions around the object and report the maximum CLIP similarity score between the rendered image and the input text prompt. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asset-Level Policy</span>, we set the maximum number of successful asset placements to 10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We further discuss and show qualitative example of the importance of leveraging the semantic knowledge in the VLM (i.e., Molmo) to predict asset placement in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Our self-improvement fine-tuning strategy demonstrates positive transfer that effectively reduces VLM’s visual hallucinations in general-domain images.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T4.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:88.8pt;vertical-align:-41.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(49.4pt,-8.8pt) scale(1.24838185996258,1.24838185996258) ;">
<table id="S4.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object HalBench</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMBER-Generative</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">CHAIRs<math id="S4.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">CHAIRi<math id="S4.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">CHAIR<math id="S4.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">COVER<math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">HAL<math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.5.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">COG<math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span></span>
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">(GPT-4o)</span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">10.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">5.4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">3.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">61.8</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">16.5</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">0.8</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span></span>
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">(GPT-4o <span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">finetuned</span>)</span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">7.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">4.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">3.2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">60.8</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">15.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;">0.7</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">ProcTHOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> also has a method to place small objects on surfaces of “receptacle objects”. However, rather than being placed according to a natural language prompt, objects are placed based on the frequency that a given object type appears on the receptacle in the hand-modeled AI2thor and RoboTHOR datasets. Thus, we opt to compare with a baseline inspired by ARCHITECT’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> small object placement that uses an inpainting model. Please find the details of our implementation in the supplementary material. After placing the objects, all methods are evaluated with the same CLIP similarity score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across the 15 test cases, the base receptacle objects (without any other objects) have an average CLIP score of 0.264. The baseline method produces composed assets with an average CLIP score of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.269\pm 0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.269</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.269\pm 0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, and our <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asset-Level Policy</span> produces an average score of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.282\pm 0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.282</mn><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">±</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.282\pm 0.001</annotation></semantics></math>
, demonstrating the ability to place assets on ‘‘receptacle assets’’ in a prompt-aligned and physically plausible way <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              During our evaluation process, an asset can only be placed if the resulting scene is physically plausible (i.e., no mesh collision).</span></span></span> Qualitatively, we observe that the baseline inpainting-based method regularly generates many overlapping objects and cannot place an item behind another. Our method, however, has access to multiple views during the iterative process and can thus spread out the object placement more naturally. Refer to Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Asset-Level Policy Evaluation ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> for qualitative comparisons.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06484v1/inpaint_iterative_comparison.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/258;" width="685" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between the baseline inpainting method (left) and <span id="S4.F7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span>’s <span id="S4.F7.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asset-Level Policy</span> (right).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Downstream application: Pretraining Florence-v2 on generated datasets and reporting fine-tuned ImageNet performance.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T5.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:447.2pt;height:89.1pt;vertical-align:-40.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(70.0pt,-13.9pt) scale(1.4558124749628,1.4558124749628) ;">
