</span></div>
</figure><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>The training GPU hours for the original Florence-v2 is estimated.</span></span></span>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Downstream Applications</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span>’s capability in scaling up 3D data, we conduct large-scale pretraining of vision encoders on the generated synthetic data. We render millions of images from generated scenes using Omniverse to create a large-scale dataset and compare feature extractors trained on our synthetic data with those trained on HyperSim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, an artist-crafted hyper-realistic dataset, along with a real-world dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we first train a vision encoder using the Florence 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> framework with a DaViT-B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> backbone. Next, we initialize an image classification model from the pretrained checkpoint and finetune the model on ImageNet1K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. The only variable in our experiments is the selection of the pretraining dataset. We convert Hypersim into Florence 2 format with three tasks: 2D object detection, semantic segmentation, and bounding box to segmentation. Doing so results in just over 860K labels, and we also generate a dataset consisting of the same number of labels using <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span>. Finally, as <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> has the advantage of scaling datasets with ease, we generate an additional 11.5M labels to perform pretraining with.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All results are provided in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Asset-Level Policy Evaluation ‣ 4.1 Simulation-Ready 3D Environment Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ 3D-GENERALIST: Vision-Language-Action Models for Crafting 3D Worlds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and compared to finetuning on the publicly available DaViT-B weights from Florence 2, which is trained on 5B real-world labels. All experiments are conducted on a 96×H100s cluster, with the runs below 1 million labels taking roughly 96 hours and the larger run of 12 million labels taking 200 hours. With commonsense and visual spatial reasoning of VLMs, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> produces high-fidelity scenes that better reflect real-world object distribution and layouts. This leads to more effective transfer on real-world downstream tasks. Furthermore, our synthetically generated environments can effectively scale up training data for vision models, achieving comparable or superior performance to manually curated datasets such as Hypersim while significantly reducing data collection effort.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D-Generalist</span>, a framework that formulates text-driven 3D world creation as a sequential decision-making problem.
The framework can be extended to integrate with other generative methods, such as image-to-3D reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, image-to-PBR material generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> or 3D asset generation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, enabling a fully generative graphics pipeline. Beyond content creation, the use of explicit 3D representations also facilitates generative simulation—enabling the automatic generation of environments for training RL policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
Limitations of <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> are further discussed in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the demonstrated efficacy of <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> in scaling up the generation of data grounded in 3D environments, <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">3D-Generalist</span> has its limitations in generating <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functional</span> layouts. An important future direction is to address how we can better generate layouts of a 3D space and consider the functional and contextual use of objects in a space based on the given prompt.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Acknowledgments.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We particularly thank Sharon Lee, Rao Fu, and Jianhe Yuan for useful discussions, as well as Ritesh Chavan and Kevin Kuo for their support.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
